# G011 — Alarm Clock

*v2. See `alarm-clock.md` for prose: the crossing from computation
into temporality, scheduled obligation as a new primitive, the vault
temporal chain IS the scheduler, event-driven semantics layered over
on-demand evaluation.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Morning Operations Alarm

[morning-ops
    IN ORDER TO
        let a single 07:00 alarm kick off three concurrent reports
        so a time trigger becomes the starting gun of a multi-agent dance

    at [[07:00]] [
        concurrent [
            @kathryn{report: "overnight P&L"}
        |
            @eliana{report: "system health"}
        |
            @sarah{report: "today's schedule"}
        ]
        join
        -> [ui[morning-dashboard]]
    ]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[alarm
    at              {time, label}            ;; register obligation
    cancel          {label}
    check           {now}                     ;; -> [fired]
    pending
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: obligation registered `where` time has not arrived yet

[alarm[at {time: @t, label: @l}]
    IN ORDER TO
        register with the temporal chain rather than evaluate now
        so the control flow inverts from pull (call-and-return)
        to push (time fires the expression at @t)

    stored in @schedule:pending
        where now is less than @t
            -> waiting
        where now is at least @t
            -> fire, append to @schedule:history
]

The resolver's job changes from "evaluate now" to "schedule for
later." Same shift that happens moving from synchronous functions
to asynchronous event handlers.

---

## Demo

[demo
    [alarm[at {time: [[07:00]], label: "wake-up"}]]
    [alarm[at {time: [[08:30]], label: "standup"}]]
    [alarm[at {time: [[23:00]], label: "nightly-summary"}]]

    -> [alarm[check {now: [[07:15]]}]]
    ;; -> [{label: "wake-up", at: 07:00}]

    [alarm[cancel {label: "standup"}]]
    -> [alarm[pending]]
    ;; -> [wake-up (already fired), nightly-summary]
]
